): I'm very glad you returned the young woman to her proper place. Under normal circumstances that would be the end of it and we'd all live happily ever after, if perhaps a little poorer in her uncle's case.

As it turns out, these are anything but normal circumstances. His niece wasn't kidnapped by a pirate captain; she eloped with him. Rubin Sozar had promised her that once he had finished some business nearby, they would start a new life together and that she would be treated like a princess. It's all a lie, of course - these people are no more capable of civilized behavior or affection than some wild animal would be.

You have to find that pirate and kill him, or he will simply trick the poor, deluded girl into eloping again. Her uncle assures me that she was treated horribly at the hands of these pirates, so badly, in fact, that he has been forced to lock her up in a section of his quarters and refuse to let anyone speak to her. He says she will remain there until she comes to her senses.

